        //   Wait, if we start from all assignment nodes, and we don't allow entering any assignment node (except the start), then:
        //   - We start BFS from all nodes with f=1.
        //   - We mark them as visited.
        //   - When we go to a neighbor, if the neighbor is an assignment node, we skip it (because we can't have an assignment node in the middle).
        //   - Otherwise, we visit it and continue.
        //   This will mark all nodes that are reachable from an assignment node without passing through any other assignment node.
        //   Note: The assignment nodes themselves are marked as reachable (from themselves).

        // So, interesting[v] = canReachUse[v] && canReachAssignClean[v]

        // Let's verify with samples.
        // Sample 1:
        // n=4, m=3
        // f: 1 0 0 2
        // Edges: 1->2, 2->3, 3->4
        // canReachUse:
        //   Start from 4 (f=2).
